A profile keeps its extensions and protected settings when the Safe Storage key changes. Chrome protects tracked
preferences (every installed extension's entry in extensions.settings, developer mode, pinned tabs, startup pages)
with two hashes: a legacy HMAC from a seed and the machine id, and an encrypted hash made with the Safe Storage key.
Once a preference has an encrypted hash, only that hash counts, so a profile whose hashes were encrypted with an
earlier key (an item recreated before chromium-safe-storage-no-silent-reset.patch, Start Over with a New Key, a
profile copied from another keychain) had every extension removed from extensions.settings and developer mode
turned off at its next load (FB-5GA3: Default, the profile loaded at startup, lost its extensions while the others
kept theirs). Now an encrypted hash that doesn't verify falls back to that preference's HMAC: if the HMAC holds, the
value is unchanged and Chrome stores a new encrypted hash with the current key; if it doesn't, the value resets as
before. Any failed encrypted hash falls back, not only one that can't be decrypted, because macOS's AES-128-CBC
with a wrong key (and Chrome's empty-key retry) sometimes decrypts to garbage with valid padding. This trusts
nothing Chrome doesn't already trust: a preference with no encrypted hash is checked by its HMAC alone, so removing
the encrypted hashes passes the same check, and kDisallowLegacyPrefMacFallback still turns every HMAC fallback off.

